; core/webrtc -- WebRTC signaling (SDP offer/answer and ICE candidate exchange)
; relayed over an existing wire-mesh connection so two peers can establish a
; direct browser-to-browser RTCDataChannel and stop relaying frames through
; whatever connection carried the signaling. Like core/exec, control travels
; as manage-command, never as a dedicated frame kind: a manage-request
; already travels over one Connection to one specific peer, so which peer a
; signal is with needs no wire field. What does need a field is which
; negotiation: request-id only correlates one manage-request/manage-response
; RPC round trip (the ack that a signal was received), not the multi-message
; offer/answer/candidate* exchange that makes up one negotiation attempt --
; especially since the answer and candidates arrive back as fresh
; manage-requests from the other side's own request-id counter, with no
; wire-level link to the offerer's own request-id. negotiation-id closes
; that gap: chosen by the offering side, echoed by the answering side, so a
; single connection can host more than one concurrent or superseding
; negotiation without ambiguity.
;
; Authorisation: webrtc:signal is gated the same way core/exec's verbs are
; (see registry/core-capabilities.md) -- accepting a signal causes the
; receiver to allocate an RTCPeerConnection and disclose its own ICE
; candidates (which can include LAN-local addresses) to whoever it answers,
; the same class of receiver-side effect exec:pty/exec:proc exist to gate,
; unlike ping/gossip which are frame kinds with no manage-command shape at
; all and no comparable receiver-side effect.

$manage-command-params /= webrtc-offer / webrtc-answer / webrtc-ice-candidate / sfu-track-map

; core/webrtc's signaling exchange is sufficient, unmodified, for a client to
; negotiate its one connection to an SFU: the SFU is just the client's one
; remote peer, per the module header above. The gap only appears once that
; connection is carrying more than one other participant's media multiplexed
; onto SDP mids: raw SDP has no way to say "mid 2 is device X's video", so
; without a spec-level answer every SFU backend would invent its own
; convention and a client built against one would not interoperate with
; another. sfu-track-map closes that gap: sent by an SFU to a connected
; client once a negotiation completes (the initial track set) and again as a
; full resend, never an incremental diff, since this is an infrequent
; membership-change event rather than a hot path, whenever call membership
; changes. It carries no authorisation of its own beyond webrtc:signal
; (the same verb already gating the negotiation this mapping describes):
; an SFU that can shape the negotiation can already trivially misreport
; whose media is on which mid, so a separate capability here would guard
; nothing a client doesn't already have to trust the SFU for as a
; facilitator role, routing and mapping, never content; see relay-hub, the
; threshold-signing coordinator, and discovery mailboxes for the same
; pattern.
sfu-track-map = {
  verb: "webrtc.sfu-track-map",
  "negotiation-id": uint,
  tracks: [* sfu-track-entry],
}

sfu-track-entry = {
  mid: tstr,
  member: device-id,
  kind: "audio" / "video",
}

webrtc-offer = {
  verb: "webrtc.offer",
  "negotiation-id": uint,
  sdp: tstr,
}

webrtc-answer = {
  verb: "webrtc.answer",
  "negotiation-id": uint,
  sdp: tstr,
}

; The outer `candidate` key is itself optional: its absence is the
; end-of-candidates sentinel (what the browser signals as event.candidate
; === null when ICE gathering completes) -- collapsing "null" and "absent"
; onto the wire is deliberate (nothing downstream distinguishes them).
webrtc-ice-candidate = {
  verb: "webrtc.ice-candidate",
  "negotiation-id": uint,
  ? candidate: ice-candidate-init,
}

ice-candidate-init = {
  candidate: tstr,
  ? "sdp-mid": tstr,
  ? "sdp-m-line-index": uint,
  ? "username-fragment": tstr,
}
